1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:04,280
How does architectural knowledge
safely change over time?

2
00:00:04,670 --> 00:00:08,040
Hello, I'm Russell and
welcome to episode 14 of

3
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:11,110
the Articulate Journal, where
I'm exploring what it takes

4
00:00:11,150 --> 00:00:15,120
to build an AI-native architecture
from first principles.

5
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:18,560
Earlier in the journal, I
introduced a Knowledge Model

6
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:21,940
and the idea of representing
architectural knowledge as claims.

7
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:25,920
A claim is an assertion
about an architecture.

8
00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:29,800
The order service uses PostgreSQL.

9
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:32,110
A customer API runs in AWS.

10
00:00:33,170 --> 00:00:36,580
The CRM platform is owned
by the customer experience

11
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:39,860
team. But these aren't
necessarily absolute truths.

12
00:00:40,170 --> 00:00:44,260
They are things Articulate
currently has some reason to

13
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:47,860
believe. And that creates the
question for this episode.

14
00:00:48,170 --> 00:00:51,780
What happens when new information arrives?

15
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:54,760
One of the goals for
Articulate is to create

16
00:00:54,800 --> 00:01:00,060
a living architectural model.
Architectural information might come from

17
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:05,870
a discovery conversation, an architecture
decision record, solution design,

18
00:01:05,960 --> 00:01:09,960
source code, or from the
architect directly, or eventually

19
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,800
from agents reasoning over
information Articulate already knows.

20
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:19,070
If the Knowledge Model represents
Articulate understanding of an

21
00:01:19,110 --> 00:01:24,170
architecture, now understanding
has to evolve as new information

22
00:01:24,210 --> 00:01:26,790
appears. But there's a tension here.

23
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:30,830
If the Knowledge Model cannot
change, it becomes stale.

24
00:01:31,170 --> 00:01:34,490
But if anything can
change it freely, it stops

25
00:01:34,530 --> 00:01:37,760
being trustworthy. So the
problem isn't really how do

26
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:41,710
we update architectural
knowledge. It's how do we safely

27
00:01:41,750 --> 00:01:46,750
evolve what Articulate
understands about an architecture?

28
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:53,120
Imagine Articulate currently believes
the order service uses PostgreSQL.

29
00:01:53,170 --> 00:01:56,120
There is evidence supporting that claim.

30
00:01:56,170 --> 00:01:58,860
And we have reasonably
high confidence in that.

31
00:01:59,170 --> 00:02:01,820
Then some new information arrives.

32
00:02:02,170 --> 00:02:04,780
The order service uses MongoDB.

33
00:02:05,170 --> 00:02:07,760
So what should Articulate do?

34
00:02:08,110 --> 00:02:10,840
It can't simply replace
PostgreSQL with MongoDB.

35
00:02:11,170 --> 00:02:13,860
Perhaps MongoDB describes
the future migration.

36
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:16,840
Perhaps the service currently uses both.

37
00:02:17,170 --> 00:02:18,910
Perhaps the new information is wrong.

38
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:23,200
Or perhaps PostgreSQL was
correct six months ago and

39
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:27,810
MongoDB is correct today.
That's an important distinction.

40
00:02:28,170 --> 00:02:31,790
New information isn't
automatically new truth.

41
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:34,120
It has to be understood in a context of

42
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:37,230
what Articulate already
knows. And that led me to

43
00:02:37,270 --> 00:02:39,780
the idea of proposed knowledge.

44
00:02:40,130 --> 00:02:43,890
New information doesn't directly modify
the authoritative Knowledge Model.

45
00:02:44,460 --> 00:02:46,760
Instead, it enters a process.

46
00:02:47,460 --> 00:02:52,860
Something like this. Incoming information
initially becomes proposed knowledge.

47
00:02:53,170 --> 00:02:56,920
That proposed knowledge is interpreted
and reconciled before becoming

48
00:02:57,190 --> 00:03:01,840
accepted knowledge. The exact
implementation isn't important here.

49
00:03:02,170 --> 00:03:03,910
What matters are the responsibilities.

50
00:03:04,170 --> 00:03:07,910
Articulate first needs to
understand what the new information

51
00:03:07,950 --> 00:03:11,030
is actually saying. Then
it needs to reconcile that

52
00:03:11,070 --> 00:03:13,870
information with what it already knows.

53
00:03:14,170 --> 00:03:16,860
Does it support something
we already believe?

54
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:17,950
Or does it contradict it?

55
00:03:18,170 --> 00:03:22,800
Does it supersede something that
was already previously true?

56
00:03:23,170 --> 00:03:24,880
Does it describe a future state?

57
00:03:25,170 --> 00:03:28,810
Or do we simply not
have enough evidence to

58
00:03:28,850 --> 00:03:32,850
know yet? Sometimes the
correct answer will be we

59
00:03:32,890 --> 00:03:35,900
don't know. That might
require more evidence.

60
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:39,620
It might trigger further
discovery, it might require an

61
00:03:39,660 --> 00:03:40,960
architect to make that judgement.

62
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,420
I don't want an agent manufacturing
architectural certainty simply

63
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:47,960
because the underlying data
model expects one answer.

64
00:03:48,170 --> 00:03:52,740
Uncertainty is a part of
architectural knowledge.

65
00:03:53,170 --> 00:03:56,870
Time becomes particularly
important during reconciliation.

66
00:03:57,130 --> 00:04:01,240
We might eventually understand
that the order service used

67
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:05,220
PostgreSQL, the order service
currently uses MongoDB and the

68
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:07,800
order service might move to DynamoDB.

69
00:04:08,170 --> 00:04:12,890
Those aren't contradictory claims.
Together they describe the evolution

70
00:04:12,930 --> 00:04:16,960
of the architecture of once was and as is

71
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
and what is to be.

72
00:04:19,170 --> 00:04:22,900
Importantly, accepting MongoDB
doesn't mean deleting PostgreSQL.

73
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:27,710
If PostgreSQL generally represents the
architecture previously, it remains

74
00:04:27,750 --> 00:04:29,860
part of the architectural history.

75
00:04:30,170 --> 00:04:33,870
This matters because architectural
decisions have contexts.

76
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:36,960
A decision made two years
ago should be understood

77
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,830
against the architecture
that existed two years ago.

78
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:44,150
Something that looks like
a poor decision today may

79
00:04:44,190 --> 00:04:47,460
have been completely reasonable
given the constraints knowledge and

80
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:49,970
the architecture that existed at the time.

81
00:04:50,170 --> 00:04:54,670
So the knowledge hasn't
simply been corrected.

82
00:04:55,170 --> 00:05:00,510
Our understanding has evolved. This
is where architectural baselines

83
00:05:00,550 --> 00:05:05,520
become important. Understanding the
baseline architecture is something that

84
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:09,550
architects say a lot. Without
articulating, I think a

85
00:05:09,590 --> 00:05:12,800
baseline gives a phrase
a specific meaning.

86
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:17,140
A baseline represents an accepted
architectural viewpoint or a

87
00:05:17,180 --> 00:05:19,910
particular point in the
architecture's evolution.

88
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:23,840
It doesn't need to duplicate
the entire Knowledge Model.

89
00:05:24,170 --> 00:05:28,980
The Knowledge Model remains one
connected body of architectural

90
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:33,270
knowledge. The baseline determines
the viewpoint through which that

91
00:05:33,310 --> 00:05:37,950
knowledge is understood. A current
baseline represents the architecture

92
00:05:37,990 --> 00:05:39,840
we understand to exist today.

93
00:05:40,050 --> 00:05:45,020
A previous baseline allows us
to understand the architecture

94
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:47,440
as it once was. And a target baseline can

95
00:05:47,480 --> 00:05:50,850
represent the architecture
we're intending to create.

96
00:05:51,170 --> 00:05:54,680
This gives us that once was, as is and

97
00:05:54,720 --> 00:06:00,120
to be states, without maintaining
three disconnected architecture models.

98
00:06:00,170 --> 00:06:04,220
And because the underlying claims,
evidence and decisions remain

99
00:06:04,260 --> 00:06:07,960
connected, we can ask
more than what did the

100
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,560
architecture look like.
We can also ask, why did

101
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:14,960
it look like that? Or
what changed between these

102
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,390
baselines? That's much
closer to how I want to

103
00:06:18,430 --> 00:06:21,800
articulate to understand
architectural change.

104
00:06:22,170 --> 00:06:24,840
There's another consequence of this model.

105
00:06:25,170 --> 00:06:29,800
Proposed knowledge might remain
proposed for quite some time.

106
00:06:30,180 --> 00:06:35,810
Imagine a significant architectural
proposal that requires human approval.

107
00:06:36,150 --> 00:06:39,210
The proposal was created and
reconciled against the current

108
00:06:39,250 --> 00:06:43,200
baseline. It looks reasonable,
but the architect doesn't approve

109
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:44,830
it till two weeks later.

110
00:06:45,170 --> 00:06:48,840
During those two weeks, the
architecture may have changed.

111
00:06:49,170 --> 00:06:50,930
Another decision may have been accepted.

112
00:06:51,460 --> 00:06:55,180
Dependency may have
changed. A system may now be

113
00:06:55,220 --> 00:06:59,790
scheduled for assignment. New
evidence may have appeared.

114
00:07:00,120 --> 00:07:02,390
The proposal was valid
against the baseline from two

115
00:07:02,430 --> 00:07:05,570
weeks ago. That doesn't
necessarily mean it's valid against

116
00:07:05,610 --> 00:07:09,760
the current baseline. So approval
alone isn't necessary enough

117
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:13,880
to make something accepted
as architectural knowledge.

118
00:07:14,180 --> 00:07:18,060
Before the proposal was
merged, it may need to

119
00:07:18,100 --> 00:07:21,770
be reconciled against the latest baseline.

120
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:25,100
I think this is particularly
interesting after the previous

121
00:07:25,140 --> 00:07:28,800
episodes on durable execution.
The execution can pause.

122
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:31,390
A human can take days to respond, but the

123
00:07:31,430 --> 00:07:34,800
architecture doesn't
pause while we're waiting.

124
00:07:35,170 --> 00:07:38,820
When execution resumes,
the world may have changed.

125
00:07:39,170 --> 00:07:43,960
There's one important distinction.
Articulate isn't ultimately going to

126
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,840
present architects with
thousands of disconnected claims.

127
00:07:47,170 --> 00:07:51,840
Claims are the assertions underneath
the architecture understanding.

128
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:55,800
Imagine Articulate has
accepted these claims.

129
00:07:56,460 --> 00:07:59,640
Order service runs in AWS ECS.

130
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:04,760
Order service reads and
writes to the order database.

131
00:08:05,170 --> 00:08:09,490
The order database is PostgreSQL,
and the order database

132
00:08:09,530 --> 00:08:14,840
runs in AWS RDS. Individually,
these are architecture assertions.

133
00:08:15,180 --> 00:08:17,840
Together, they begin to describe a system.

134
00:08:18,170 --> 00:08:23,600
They identify architectural entities,
they establish relationships, and they

135
00:08:23,640 --> 00:08:26,800
create a connected architecture model.

136
00:08:27,170 --> 00:08:32,000
Other claims might describe ownership,
business capabilities, characteristics,

137
00:08:32,180 --> 00:08:37,010
principles, and decisions. The
architecture emerges from the

138
00:08:37,050 --> 00:08:39,920
relationships between what
Articulate has learnt.

139
00:08:40,170 --> 00:08:43,920
But the underlying claim remains
important because they explain

140
00:08:43,960 --> 00:08:48,840
why Articulate believes
those relationships exist.

141
00:08:49,170 --> 00:08:52,160
What evidence supports them,
where they came from, and

142
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:55,430
how confident we are. And
when they're believed to

143
00:08:55,470 --> 00:08:59,840
be true, the architecture
model gives us understanding.

144
00:09:00,170 --> 00:09:03,800
The claims give us the
understanding context.

145
00:09:04,170 --> 00:09:06,280
This has changed how I
think about the Knowledge

146
00:09:06,320 --> 00:09:09,380
Model. Originally, the problem
sounded like a data modeling

147
00:09:09,420 --> 00:09:13,850
problem. How should Articulate
store architectural information?

148
00:09:14,170 --> 00:09:17,810
But storage isn't really
the interesting problem.

149
00:09:18,130 --> 00:09:22,160
The interesting problem is maintaining
an evolving understanding of

150
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:25,920
the architecture. New information
has to be interpreted.

151
00:09:26,080 --> 00:09:28,840
It has to be reconciled with
the existing understanding.

152
00:09:29,170 --> 00:09:30,790
Time has to be considered.

153
00:09:31,460 --> 00:09:33,840
Evidence and confidence have to matter.

154
00:09:34,170 --> 00:09:38,770
And sometimes, uncertainty
has to remain unresolved.

155
00:09:39,170 --> 00:09:42,840
This also clarifies an important
architectural boundary.

156
00:09:43,170 --> 00:09:45,860
Agents shouldn't simply
modify the Knowledge Model.

157
00:09:46,170 --> 00:09:48,950
They can contribute information.
They can interpret it.

158
00:09:49,170 --> 00:09:51,980
They can reconcile claims.
They can gather evidence.

159
00:09:52,170 --> 00:09:54,820
They can reason about what has changed.

160
00:09:55,170 --> 00:09:59,850
But changing authoritative architectural
knowledge is a governed behavior.

161
00:10:00,180 --> 00:10:03,730
There's not simply a database update.

162
00:10:04,460 --> 00:10:08,800
Knowledge evolution is in itself
an architectural capability.

163
00:10:09,100 --> 00:10:11,590
So, we now have a model
where Articulate can

164
00:10:11,630 --> 00:10:16,530
continually receive new architectural
information without automatically treating

165
00:10:16,570 --> 00:10:21,810
that information as truth. New
information becomes proposed knowledge.

166
00:10:22,090 --> 00:10:25,880
It is interpreted and reconciled
against existing understanding.

167
00:10:26,460 --> 00:10:30,650
Accepted knowledge can evolve our
understanding of the architecture

168
00:10:30,690 --> 00:10:33,800
without rewriting what came before it.

169
00:10:34,170 --> 00:10:38,600
And baselines allow us to
understand the architecture from

170
00:10:38,640 --> 00:10:40,850
different points in its evolution.

171
00:10:41,170 --> 00:10:45,460
Here's another problem. A
proposed change can be perfectly

172
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:49,780
consistent with what Articulate
already knows and still be

173
00:10:49,820 --> 00:10:54,060
a bad architectural change. It
might violate an architectural

174
00:10:54,100 --> 00:10:57,840
principle. It might introduce
a dependency on a system

175
00:10:57,880 --> 00:10:59,840
that we're trying to retire.

176
00:11:00,110 --> 00:11:05,080
It might improve availability while
significantly increasing operational

177
00:11:05,120 --> 00:11:08,860
complexity. Knowing what the
architecture is isn't enough.

178
00:11:09,170 --> 00:11:12,920
We also need to evaluate
what knowledge means.

179
00:11:13,170 --> 00:11:15,860
And that is the question
for the next episode.

180
00:11:16,170 --> 00:11:20,660
How can Articulate continuously
evaluate an architecture rather than

181
00:11:20,700 --> 00:11:25,810
simply storing it? That's where
architectural assurance begins.

182
00:11:26,170 --> 00:11:27,790
See you on the next one.
